  "text": "Allergies:\nNo Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions\n\nChief Complaint:\nworsening DOE\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ year-old lady with PMHx sarcoidosis (has home oxygen prn), \nand a history of DVT now presenting with shortness of breath. \nPatient reports worsening dyspnea & cough on exertion over the \npast 5 days at home with associated chest pressure. Reports \nbeing 85% on RA when she went for an appt at ___ on ___. She \ndenies any heart palpitations or hemotypsis.  \n\nIn the ED, initial VS were: 99.6 96 116/83 30 89% 2L. Labs were \nnotable for WBC 8.2 with normal diff, and chemistry panel WNL; \ntroponin was <0.01. Blood cultures were sent. UA showed spec \n___ > 1.050; no evidence of infection. CXR (portable, AP) \nshowed perihilar opacities possibly related to sarcoidosis, \nscarring at LL base, some left hemidiaphragm elevation, and \npossible superimposed edema. CTA chest showed no evidence of PE, \nbut did show bronchiectasis, honeycombing and GGOs in the RLL, \nconcerning for an infectious process. She was given \nipratropium/albuterol nebs x2, aspirin 325 mg PO, and \nlevofloxacin 750 mg IV. VS on transfer: 98 89 129/58 22 98% 2L \nNC.  \nOn arrival to the floor, the patient appears comfortable and is \nable to speak in full sentences.  \n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS:  \n(+) Per HPI, cough, shortness of breath, chest tightness with \ncoughing/exertion, urinary incontinence (baseline)  \n(-) Denies fever, chills, night sweats. Denies headache, sinus \ntenderness, rhinorrhea or congestion or wheezing. Denies chest \npain, chest pressure, palpitations, or weakness. Denies nausea, \nvomiting, diarrhea, constipation, abdominal pain, or changes in \nbowel habits. Denies dysuria, frequency, or urgency. Denies \narthralgias or myalgias. Denies rashes or skin changes.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nMs. ___ is a ___ year-old woman with PMHx sarcoidosis (has \nhome oxygen prn), and a history of DVT, presented with hypoxia, \nworsening dyspnea on exertion & cough over the past 5 days, \nbeing treated for presumed RLL PNA vs worsening of underlying \nsarcoidosis given was off meds for 3 weeks 5 weeks ago. \nDischarged home on continuous oxygen with pulmonary follow up.   \n\n \n# DOE/hypoxia: On admission there was concern for RLL PNA given \nCTA findings though were ___. She was afebrile without \nleukocytosis however given her poor lung reserve to start with \ngiven her pulmonary sarcoidosis she was treated with 5 day \ncourse of levaquin that she completed while in the hospital. She \nreported feeling better with the antibiotic and re-initiation \nher home bronchodilators along with nebulizers (she self \ndiscontinued her home bronchodilators at home). EKG was not \nconcerning for ischemic event along with Troponin x2 <0.01. CTA \nwas negative for pulmonary embolism. There was also a question \nwhether her underlying pulmonary sarcoidosis worsened given she \nwas off her medication for 3 weeks 5 weeks ago with \nre-institution of these medications only 2 weeks prior to \nadmission after her LLE ulcers were found to be healing (she was \noff the meds to give time for ulcers to heal). She missed her \n___ methotrexate dose. Other consideration is cardiac sarcoid \ngiven chest pressure accompanying her DOE almost everytime it \noccurs. She completed course of levofloxacin 750mg PO daily (___) for 5 day course through ___. She was on standing \nalbuterol/ipratropium nebs Q6H standing while in the hospital. \nWe continued home advair and spiriva. She will follow her every \n___ methotrexate (was not given as inpatient since she \nprefered to keep it every ___. Her ambulatory saturation \nwas 85% on 2 L NC and mid 90's on 4L and was advised to wear \noxygen at all times on 2 LNC and uptitrate to 4L when \nambulating. Pulm team was following with us in her care. They \nrecommended  Fluticasone Propionate 110mcg 2 PUFF inhaler twice \ndaily which was provided upon discharge along with nebulizer \nmachine for her to use at home. \n\n# Sarcoidosis: Patient take Plaquenil 400mg BID and methotrexate \n20mg ___. She missed her methotrexate dose on ___. She was \noff the methotrexate and plaquenil for 3 weeks to give time for \nher ___ ulcers to heal but was back on them per patient for the \nlast 2 weeks since she was told that her ulcers are improving \nand probably won't need a graft. She will follow her every \n___ schedule as above. To have follow up with pulm as \noutpatient. \n\n# Left lower extremity ulcer: she has 2 1 cmm shallow ulcers, 1 \nat left lateral ankle and 1 at dorsum of the foot, both with \nerythematous base. Dressing changed every ___ at ___. Will \nfollow at ___ for her ulcers. \n\n# Transitional issues:\n- Left thyroid lobe hypodensity could be further evaluated with \nultrasound. \n- outpatient pulm follow up \n- consideration of outpatient echo \n- consideration for prednisone for pulmonary sarcoidosis\n- f/u blood culture ___\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nSarcoidosis\nPossible pneumonia",
